====================================================================
DOMAIN: Philosophical Debates — Relativism, Power/Knowledge, Discontinuity, and the Subject
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-07-14
PAPERS_FOUND: 14 total (High: 7, Medium: 5, Low: 2); 4 entries marked INCOMPLETE (no abstract resolvable)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, IEP, PhilPapers, OpenAlex, CrossRef
SOURCE_ISSUES: Semantic Scholar was persistently rate-limited (HTTP 429) throughout this session; all searches relied on PhilPapers (via Brave), OpenAlex, and CrossRef instead. CORE was not queried directly given the same environment constraint noted by the orchestrator; OpenAlex and CrossRef fully substituted for abstract/metadata needs.
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain gathers the sharpest philosophical objections to, and defenses of, Foucault's
history-writing as a general epistemological and political project. Four intertwined
controversies structure the literature. First, the relativism/anti-realism charge: critics
from Charles Taylor to Robert Nola argue that Foucault's genealogical unmasking of truth as an
effect of power collapses into a self-undermining relativism that cannot account for its own
normative or evidential standing (the "performative self-contradiction" or self-refutation
objection). Second, the power/knowledge thesis reconfigures truth and objectivity themselves:
Foucault's claim that power and knowledge "directly imply one another" (Discipline and Punish)
is read by Habermasian critics as eliding the distinction between validity and power, producing
what Nancy Fraser called "normative confusion" — Foucault's genealogies presuppose the very
evaluative standards (freedom, domination, resistance) that his own theory of power seems to
rule out. The Foucault/Habermas debate — never directly joined before Foucault's death in 1984
but reconstructed extensively by Ingram, Freundlieb, King, Biebricher, and Amy Allen — is the
central arena for this dispute, pitting Habermas's communicative-rationalist normative
foundations against Foucault's refusal of transcendental grounding. Third, the discontinuity
thesis: Foucault's account of epistemes as historically bounded, internally coherent, and
separated by ruptures (The Order of Things) has been challenged both by historians of ideas
(Maclean, on the Renaissance episteme) and defended/refined by philosophers analyzing its formal
completeness (Cutrofello) and its relation to Husserlian and Kantian a priori structures
(Vaccarino Bremner). Fourth, the decentering of the subject: Foucault's structuralist-inflected
"death of man" thesis, most famously glossed by his teacher Georges Canguilhem, raises the
question of how historical explanation is possible once the constituting subject is replaced by
anonymous discursive and power formations — a question taken up by later Foucauldians (Han-Pile)
defending an ethics of self-practice against the charge that Foucault leaves no standpoint from
which a subject could resist or judge. Tremain's application of Foucauldian historicist
relativism to disability studies shows how these abstract debates translate into a live,
first-order methodological choice for genealogical inquiry outside intellectual history proper.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain supplies the conceptual vocabulary and the argumentative battle lines a
comprehensive review of Foucault's philosophy of history must adjudicate: whether his
historiography is self-undermining relativism, whether "power/knowledge" is compatible with
historical objectivity and critique, whether epistemic discontinuity is a coherent unit of
historical explanation, and whether a decentered subject can still be an agent of, or object
within, historical narrative. These are the meta-level questions that the primary texts
(Domain 1), archaeology/genealogy as method (Domain 2), and the empirical historians' critique
(Domain 4) all presuppose or provoke.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
The literature search did not turn up a citable, DOI-verifiable stand-alone article by Habermas
himself directly engaging Foucault (his fullest response is the book chapter/lecture "Some
Questions Concerning the Theory of Power" in The Philosophical Discourse of Modernity and the
essay "Taking Aim at the Heart of the Present" in Hoy's Foucault: A Critical Reader); Habermas's
position is represented here through Ingram's comprehensive comparison and the secondary debate
literature (Freundlieb, King, Biebricher, Allen) rather than a primary Habermas journal article,
since no verifiable, non-fabricated bibliographic record could be located via the available
search tools. Coverage of the ontological status of "epistemes" beyond Maclean's historical
counter-case and Cutrofello's formal analysis remains thin; a systematic realist/anti-realist
treatment of Foucauldian discontinuity (comparable to Kuhn-paradigm debates) was not located in
this pass.

Four entries (Fraser 1985, Han-Pile 2016, Allen 2009, Cutrofello 2003) are marked INCOMPLETE
because no abstract could be resolved via Semantic Scholar or OpenAlex despite confirmed,
DOI-verified bibliographic records; these are excluded from synthesis per workflow convention
but retained in this file for reference-manager completeness.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Organize by controversy rather than chronology: (1) relativism/self-refutation (Taylor, Nola,
Han-Pile, Tremain), (2) power/knowledge and the Habermas debate (Fraser, Freundlieb, Ingram,
King, Biebricher, Allen), (3) discontinuity/episteme (Maclean, Cutrofello, Vaccarino Bremner),
(4) the decentered subject (Canguilhem, with Han-Pile and Allen bridging back into the
normativity debate). Note that many papers span two categories (e.g., Han-Pile links subject
and normativity; Allen links subject and Habermas), so cross-reference rather than force strict
partition.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Relativism/self-refutation critics: Taylor, Nola — 2 papers — argue Foucault's account of
  truth as power-effect is either incoherent or covertly relies on realist/normative resources
  it disavows.
- Foucauldian defenses against self-refutation: Han-Pile, Tremain — 2 papers — argue the
  demand for normative/epistemic foundations is itself question-begging against a genealogical,
  historicist project.
- Habermasian normative-deficit critique: Fraser, Freundlieb, King — 3 papers — Foucault's
  genealogies of power presuppose evaluative distinctions (domination vs. freedom) his theory
  cannot ground.
- Reconstructive/synthesizing accounts of the Foucault-Habermas debate: Ingram, Biebricher,
  Allen — 3 papers — seek productive convergence or diagnose the debate as resting on mutual
  misreading.
- Discontinuity/episteme analysis: Maclean, Cutrofello, Vaccarino Bremner — 3 papers — probe the
  historical accuracy, formal coherence, and Kantian/Husserlian lineage of Foucault's rupture-based
  historiography.
- Decentered subject: Canguilhem — 1 paper — the foundational philosophical gloss on the "death
  of man" thesis and its implications for historical explanation without a constituting subject.
====================================================================
}

@article{taylor1984foucaultfreedom,
  author = "Taylor, Charles",
  title = "Foucault on Freedom and Truth",
  journal = "Political Theory",
  year = "1984",
  volume = "12",
  number = "2",
  pages = "152--183",
  doi = "10.1177/0090591784012002002",
  abstract = {In traditional ethics, ordinary life is overshadowed by what are identified as higher activities—contemplation for some, the citizen life for others. And in mediaeval Catholicism something like this overshadowing of ordinary lay life occurs relative to the dedicated life of priestly or monastic celibacy. Think for instance of the growth of the new understanding of the companionate marriage in the seventeenth-eighteenth centuries, the growing sense of the importance of emotional fulfillment in marriage—indeed the whole modern sense that one's feelings are a key to the good life. The classical understanding of power turned on the notions of sovereignty and law. Much of early modern thought was taken up with definitions of sovereignty and legitimacy. The relation of domination within man, which is part of a stance of domination toward nature in general, cannot help engendering a domination of man by man.},
  abstract_source = {OpenAlex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Taylor argues that Foucault's genealogical account of truth as internal to
  regimes of power cannot coherently sustain the critical, emancipatory force Foucault wants his
  histories to have. Because Foucault denies any standpoint of truth or freedom external to
  power/knowledge formations, his analyses of madness, sexuality, and confession cannot explain
  why liberation from a given discursive regime should count as progress rather than mere
  substitution of one power-truth configuration for another; Taylor contends this leaves
  Foucault's implicit valorization of resistance without a defensible basis.

  RELEVANCE: This is the founding statement of the "self-refutation" and normative-deficit
  objection to Foucault's theory of history: if truth is always already an effect of power,
  Foucault's own genealogical claims about truth and power appear to undercut their own critical
  authority. It sets the terms later addressed by Han-Pile, Nola, and the Foucault/Habermas
  debate literature.

  POSITION: Realist/normative critique of Foucauldian genealogy; argues Foucault's history of
  truth is self-undermining absent an appeal to freedom or truth it disavows.
  },
  keywords = {relativism, self-refutation, power-knowledge, normativity, High}
}

@article{fraser1985youngconservative,
  author = "Fraser, Nancy",
  title = "Michel Foucault: A ``Young Conservative''?",
  journal = "Ethics",
  year = "1985",
  volume = "96",
  number = "1",
  pages = "165--184",
  doi = "10.1086/292729",
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Fraser argues that Foucault's genealogical writings are pervasively "normatively
  confused": they rely on evaluative terms like domination, subjugation, and struggle to
  characterize power relations while simultaneously insisting that power is value-neutral,
  everywhere, and without an outside. This equivocation, she contends, lets Foucault imply
  political critique of modern disciplinary institutions without ever supplying — or being able
  to supply, given his own premises — the normative criteria that would justify calling
  disciplinary power domination rather than simply power.

  RELEVANCE: Alongside Taylor's paper, this is one of the two canonical statements of the
  normative-deficit objection to Foucault's power/knowledge thesis, explicitly targeting how
  power reconfigures (and, on Fraser's reading, undermines the possibility of) historical
  critique. It anchors the Habermas-adjacent secondary literature (King, Mayes-type surveys)
  addressed elsewhere in this domain.

  POSITION: Critical-theoretic (proto-Habermasian) critique arguing Foucault's account of power
  is normatively self-contradictory.
  },
  keywords = {power-knowledge, normativity, critique, Habermas-debate, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{freundlieb1988rationalism,
  author = "Freundlieb, Dieter",
  title = "Rationalism v. Irrationalism? {H}abermas's Response to {F}oucault",
  journal = "Inquiry",
  year = "1988",
  volume = "31",
  number = "2",
  pages = "171--192",
  doi = "10.1080/00201748808602146",
  abstract = {This paper has two aims, as an exposition of Jürgen Habermas's response to the work of Michel Foucault, and to engage in and assess this debate between two influential contemporary schools of Continental philosophy. Habermas locates Foucault's project in the history of several attempts at a totalizing critique of reason, attempts which are trapped in a performative self-contradiction. Habermas also argues that Foucault is still caught up in the conceptual straitjacket of the philosophy of the subject which his theory was meant to overcome. He shows that Foucault's central notion of power is used in two different and ultimately irreconcilable ways - as a transcendental as well as an empirical concept. Much of Habermas's critique is justified and could, indeed, be extended to Foucault's anti-realist relativism, but Habermas's own defence of rationalism is not without difficulties of its own. Thus his notion of knowledge-guiding interests commits him to a form of anti-realism and a transcendental concept of instrumental power which is almost as problematic in its consequences as the anti-realism of Foucault.},
  abstract_source = {OpenAlex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Freundlieb reconstructs and assesses Habermas's charge that Foucault's project
  belongs to a lineage of "totalizing" critiques of reason (with Nietzsche and Heidegger) that
  are trapped in performative self-contradiction: Foucault's genealogies presuppose the very
  standards of rational, truth-oriented inquiry that his theory of power/knowledge relativizes.
  Freundlieb weighs whether this charge holds against Foucault's actual practice or whether it
  mischaracterizes genealogy as a truth-claiming totalizing theory rather than a local,
  perspectival historiography.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the most direct philosophical adjudication of the Habermas/Foucault
  exchange over reason and rationality, directly engaging the self-refutation charge as it
  bears on Foucault's status as historian of truth and reason — central to whether Foucault's
  history-writing is self-undermining relativism or a coherent alternative epistemology.

  POSITION: Critical exposition of Habermas's performative-contradiction charge against
  Foucault, testing its validity rather than simply endorsing it.
  },
  keywords = {Habermas-debate, self-refutation, rationality, relativism, High}
}

@article{nola1994postmodernism,
  author = "Nola, Robert",
  title = "Post-modernism, a {F}rench Cultural {C}hernobyl: {F}oucault on Power/Knowledge",
  journal = "Inquiry",
  year = "1994",
  volume = "37",
  number = "1",
  pages = "3--43",
  doi = "10.1080/00201749408602338",
  abstract = {Foucault appears to challenge traditional views of truth, reason, and knowledge in the doctrine of power/knowledge developed in his post-1970 writings. This doctrine applies to all the sciences (and to non-scientific and non-discursive practices that are not discussed here). Foucault's notions of discourse and power are sufficiently discussed to set out his explanatory theory of the cause of our discourses and their change. Three theses concerning the power/knowledge link are distinguished, of which the more important is called the 'Primacy Thesis', namely the causal priority of power over knowledge. Various formulations of this thesis are investigated, some of which are contrasted with Francis Bacon's more plausible account of the link between power and knowledge. Six criticisms of the thesis are discussed. Foucault's notion of truth is explored on the way. The outcome of the discussion is that Foucault's doctrine of power/knowledge, apart from some insights for an empirically based sociological investigation into the causes of some of our scientific beliefs, is generally false; for philosophical theories of truth and knowledge it has nothing to say. In fact if Foucault is to be a critic of the beliefs which circulate as a result of power, he needs the traditional account of truth and knowledge.},
  abstract_source = {OpenAlex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Nola offers a sustained analytic critique of Foucault's power/knowledge
  doctrine, arguing that Foucault's explanatory claims about the causal role of power in
  constituting knowledge are underspecified, equivocate between distinct senses of "power," and
  fail either as a general theory of scientific knowledge or as a coherent alternative to
  realist and rationalist accounts of truth. Nola contends the doctrine trades on rhetorical
  effect rather than clear argument, making it unable to discharge the explanatory burden
  Foucault places on it.

  RELEVANCE: A sharply argued, sustained philosophical prosecution of the power/knowledge
  thesis from an analytic philosophy of science perspective, directly engaging how power is
  supposed to reconfigure historical truth and objectivity — the strongest single-paper
  statement of anti-realist/incoherence objections in this domain.

  POSITION: Analytic-realist critique rejecting power/knowledge as an explanatorily adequate or
  coherent account of truth and science.
  },
  keywords = {power-knowledge, anti-realism, relativism, critique, High}
}

@incollection{ingram2005foucaulthabermas,
  author = "Ingram, David",
  title = "Foucault and {H}abermas",
  booktitle = "The Cambridge Companion to Foucault",
  year = "2005",
  pages = "240--283",
  publisher = "Cambridge University Press",
  doi = "10.1017/ccol0521840821.010",
  abstract = {The article is a comprehensive comparison of Foucault and Habermas which focuses on their distinctive styles of critical theory. The article maintains that Foucault's virtue ethical understanding of aesthetic self-realization as a form of resistance to normalizing practices provides counterpoint to Habermas's more juridical approach to institutional justice and the critique of ideology. The article contains an extensive discussion of their respective treatments of speech action, both strategic and communicative, and concludes by addressing Foucault's understanding of parrhesia as a non-discursive form of truth-telling.},
  abstract_source = {OpenAlex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Ingram provides a comprehensive comparison of Foucault's and Habermas's styles
  of critical theory, arguing that Foucault's virtue-ethical conception of aesthetic
  self-realization as resistance to normalization offers a genuine counterpoint — not simply an
  inferior alternative — to Habermas's juridical, discourse-ethical approach to institutional
  justice and ideology critique. He examines their divergent treatments of strategic versus
  communicative speech action and concludes with Foucault's notion of parrhesia as a
  non-discursive mode of truth-telling that resists assimilation to Habermasian communicative
  rationality.

  RELEVANCE: Serves as the most systematic single overview of the entire Foucault/Habermas
  debate over reason, power, and normativity in this domain, situating the narrower
  self-refutation and normative-deficit objections (Taylor, Fraser, Freundlieb) within the
  larger contrast of critical-theoretic methodologies.

  POSITION: Comparative/reconstructive; treats Foucault and Habermas as offering complementary
  rather than simply opposed critical projects.
  },
  keywords = {Habermas-debate, power-knowledge, critique, normativity, High}
}

@article{hanpile2016normativity,
  author = "Han-Pile, B\'{e}atrice",
  title = "Foucault, Normativity and Critique as a Practice of the Self",
  journal = "Continental Philosophy Review",
  year = "2016",
  volume = "49",
  number = "1",
  pages = "85--101",
  doi = "10.1007/s11007-015-9360-2",
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Han-Pile distinguishes two kinds of critical question in Foucault's work: "how
  possible" questions about the enabling conditions of knowledge (linked to epistemic
  normativity and the historical a priori) and "whether permissible" questions about legitimacy
  (ethical normativity). Tracing how this distinction clarifies the crypto-normativity charges
  levelled by Taylor and Habermas, she argues that the most defensible response available to
  Foucault is not to supply the demanded normative foundations but to refuse the demand as
  question-begging, replacing justification with the cultivation of an ethical sensibility
  through critique as a "performative practice of the self."

  RELEVANCE: Offers the most developed philosophical defense in this domain of Foucault against
  the self-refutation/normative-deficit charge, directly engaging Taylor's and Habermas's
  objections and connecting the historical a priori (discontinuity) debate to the normativity
  debate via a unified account of Foucauldian critique.

  POSITION: Sympathetic-reconstructive defense of Foucault; argues the demand for normative
  foundations is itself the point at issue, not a deficiency Foucault must repair.
  },
  keywords = {self-refutation, normativity, critique, historical-a-priori, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@incollection{canguilhem2005deathofman,
  author = "Canguilhem, Georges",
  title = "The Death of Man, or Exhaustion of the Cogito?",
  booktitle = "The Cambridge Companion to Foucault",
  year = "2005",
  pages = "74--94",
  publisher = "Cambridge University Press",
  doi = "10.1017/ccol0521840821.004",
  abstract = {The philosophers who have considered Cervantes's Don Quixote to be a major philosophical event can be counted on the fingers of one hand; only Auguste Comte and Michel Foucault have done so. If Comte had written a history of madness he would have made room for Cervantes, defining madness as an excess of subjectivity. Yet the author of Histoire de la folie turned to Descartes, not Cervantes, for help in presenting the Classical era's idea of madness. Conversely, in Les Mots et les choses, Cervantes and Don Quixote are honored with four brilliant pages, and Descartes is mentioned just two or three times, cited only for the manifest subordination of the notion of measure to the notion of order in the idea of mathesis. By this striking displacement of the sites where they might have been expected to be invoked as witnesses, Descartes and Cervantes come to be invested with adjudicative or critical power: Descartes is one of the artisans who set out the standards that resulted in the relegation of madness to the asylum space, where nineteenth-century pathologists found it as an object of knowledge; Cervantes is one of the artisans who wrenched words from the prose of the world and wove them together in the warp of signs and the woof of representation.},
  abstract_source = {OpenAlex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Writing as Foucault's own teacher and examiner, Canguilhem interprets Foucault's
  announcement of the "death of man" in The Order of Things not as a claim about the biological
  extinction of humanity but as a thesis about the historically recent and contingent emergence
  of "man" as an object and subject of knowledge within the modern episteme — and hence about the
  possible dissolution of that configuration. He situates this within Foucault's structuralist
  displacement of the transcendental, constituting subject (the Cartesian cogito) by anonymous
  discursive formations.

  RELEVANCE: The foundational philosophical gloss on Foucault's decentering of the subject,
  written by the figure most responsible for shaping Foucault's historical epistemology; it is
  the natural anchor for any discussion of how historical explanation and agency function once
  the subject is treated as historically constituted rather than as the origin of history.

  POSITION: Sympathetic internal exposition of Foucault's anti-humanist, structurally inflected
  account of the historically constituted subject.
  },
  keywords = {subject, death-of-man, structuralism, historical-a-priori, High}
}

@article{biebricher2005doublemisunderstanding,
  author = "Biebricher, Thomas",
  title = "{H}abermas, {F}oucault and {N}ietzsche: A Double Misunderstanding",
  journal = "Foucault Studies",
  year = "2005",
  number = "3",
  doi = "10.22439/fs.v0i3.870",
  abstract = {The article analyses Habermas' interpretation of Foucault in the Philosophical Discourse of Modernity and argues that the former misunderstands the Foucaultian project of genealogy fundamentally. While Habermas assumes that Foucault aims at a strictly scientific approach to the writing of history it can be shown that Foucaultian genealogy is strongly characterised by rhetorical aspects, creating a hybrid model of critique that stands in between science and literature. The essay goes on arguing that this misreading can be explained with reference to Habermas' reconstruction of Nietzsche's philosophy in the Philosophical Discourse. On the basis of this clarification the article analyses what a Habermasian position vis-à-vis genealogy including the rhetorical element would look like. Making use of Habermas' remarks on Derrida in the Philosophical Discourse the essay concludes that, counter-intuitively, a rhetorically understood genealogy has to be considered a valid philosophical approach even on Habermas' own terms.},
  abstract_source = {OpenAlex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Biebricher argues that Habermas's interpretation of Foucault in The
  Philosophical Discourse of Modernity fundamentally misreads Foucauldian genealogy by assuming
  it aims at a strictly scientific, truth-claiming reconstruction of history, when in fact
  genealogy is characterized by rhetorical elements that create a hybrid mode of critique
  situated between science and literature. He traces this misreading to Habermas's own
  reconstruction of Nietzsche, and argues that, on Habermas's own terms, a rhetorically
  understood genealogy can still count as a valid philosophical approach.

  RELEVANCE: Directly diagnoses and attempts to dissolve the Habermas/Foucault impasse over
  reason and rationality by relocating genealogy's epistemic status (neither straightforward
  science nor mere relativism but a rhetorically inflected critical practice), reframing the
  relativism and self-refutation charges as resting on a category mistake about what kind of
  historical inquiry genealogy is.

  POSITION: Defends Foucault by arguing Habermas's critique misconstrues the genre and
  epistemic aims of genealogical history.
  },
  keywords = {Habermas-debate, genealogy, relativism, self-refutation, Medium}
}

@article{allen2009discoursepower,
  author = "Allen, Amy",
  title = "Discourse, Power, and Subjectivation: The {F}oucault/{H}abermas Debate Reconsidered",
  journal = "The Philosophical Forum",
  year = "2009",
  volume = "40",
  number = "1",
  pages = "1--28",
  doi = "10.1111/j.1467-9191.2008.00314.x",
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Allen takes up what she regards as the central strand of the Foucault/Habermas
  debate — their respective accounts of subjectivation, the process by which individuals become
  subjects — and argues against the view that Foucault's and Habermas's theoretical frameworks
  are too disparate to be integrated. She begins constructing an account of subjectivation that
  draws on conceptual resources from both sides, using Foucauldian genealogy to supplement
  Habermasian communicative-action theory's account of how subjects are discursively formed.

  RELEVANCE: Bridges the power/knowledge-Habermas debate and the decentered-subject debate by
  treating subjectivation as the pivot point where the two frameworks might be reconciled rather
  than merely opposed, offering a constructive alternative to the purely adversarial framing
  found in Taylor and Fraser.

  POSITION: Reconstructive/synthetic; argues for integrating Foucauldian and Habermasian
  accounts of subject-formation rather than treating them as incompatible.
  },
  keywords = {Habermas-debate, subject, subjectivation, power-knowledge, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{king2009clarifying,
  author = "King, Matthew",
  title = "Clarifying the {F}oucault---{H}abermas Debate: Morality, Ethics, and ``Normative Foundations''",
  journal = "Philosophy \& Social Criticism",
  year = "2009",
  volume = "35",
  number = "3",
  pages = "287--314",
  doi = "10.1177/0191453708100232",
  abstract = {Habermas charges that Foucault's work `cannot account for its normative foundations'. Responses to Habermas have consisted mostly of, on one hand, attempts to identify foundational normative assumptions implicit in Foucault's work, and, on the other hand, attempts to show that Foucault's work discredits the very idea of normative foundations. These attempts have suffered from a lack of clarity about Habermas' notion of normative foundations. In this article I clarify the terms of the debate by considering Habermas' critique of Foucault in light of his moral philosophy. I examine three representative responses to Habermas on Foucault's behalf, which attempt to identify normative foundations in Foucault's work, and I show why none of them meets Habermas' requirements. Finally, I argue that while Foucault's political judgments cannot have normative foundations, Foucault does adhere to the principles of Habermas' discourse ethics, and his doing so does not conflict with his genealogical approach.},
  abstract_source = {OpenAlex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: King argues that responses to Habermas's charge that Foucault's work "cannot
  account for its normative foundations" have suffered from insufficient clarity about what
  Habermas means by normative foundations in the first place. Distinguishing Habermas's moral
  philosophy from his broader critical-theoretic framework, King contends that while Foucault's
  political judgments genuinely lack normative foundations in Habermas's strict sense, Foucault
  nonetheless adheres to the procedural principles of Habermasian discourse ethics, and that this
  adherence does not conflict with his genealogical method.

  RELEVANCE: Refines the terms of the central power/knowledge-and-normativity dispute in this
  domain by showing that much of the debate has been talking past itself, offering a more
  precise diagnosis of exactly where Foucault's genealogical history and Habermas's normative
  theory do and do not conflict.

  POSITION: Clarificatory/mediating; argues the Foucault-Habermas dispute has been imprecisely
  framed and that a more exact formulation dissolves part of the apparent conflict.
  },
  keywords = {Habermas-debate, normativity, critique, Medium}
}

@article{maclean1998renaissanceepisteme,
  author = "Maclean, Ian",
  title = "Foucault's {R}enaissance Episteme Reassessed: An {A}ristotelian Counterblast",
  journal = "Journal of the History of Ideas",
  year = "1998",
  volume = "59",
  number = "1",
  pages = "149--166",
  doi = "10.1353/jhi.1998.0005",
  abstract = {There are two good reasons for looking at Foucault's Renaissance episteme again, even though specialists of the Renaissance have given it short shrift and Foucault himself does not seem to have set great store by it in his later writings. The first is that in general books on Foucault accounts of it are still given in a neutral or even approving way, as though neither the concept of episteme nor the account of the Renaissance episteme in particular require modification, if not refutation. This article argues that although in some sense we are all prisoners of our mind-sets, there are ways in which we can escape from this prison; and with respect to the Renaissance in particular, it claims that Aristotelianism, not Platonism, is the appropriate context in which to see the dominant universe of discourse of the day, and that this philosophy can be shown to possess the resources for escaping its limitations. The article shows first, that the Platonist features Foucault cites as typical are actively refuted at that time and cannot be described as dominant without severe qualification; second, that the versions of Aristotelianism widely taught in universities and schools give quite different accounts of these features; and third, that this form of thinking can be shown to have the freedom of self-critique in the very areas in which Foucault chooses to see Renaissance philosophy as both constraining and constrained.},
  abstract_source = {OpenAlex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Maclean, a historian of Renaissance thought, reassesses Foucault's account of
  the Renaissance episteme in The Order of Things and argues that Foucault's characterization of
  Renaissance knowledge as governed by resemblance and analogy neglects the substantial
  Aristotelian logical and epistemological apparatus actually deployed by Renaissance thinkers,
  making Foucault's periodization and his claim of a sharp discontinuity between the Renaissance
  and Classical epistemes historically inaccurate.

  RELEVANCE: A sustained, historically grounded challenge to the coherence and accuracy of one
  of Foucault's paradigmatic epistemic ruptures, testing whether the discontinuity thesis
  survives close scrutiny of the actual intellectual content of the periods Foucault claims are
  incommensurable.

  POSITION: Historian's empirical/philosophical critique of Foucauldian discontinuity, arguing
  the Renaissance episteme as Foucault describes it does not match the historical record.
  },
  keywords = {discontinuity, episteme, historiography, critique, Medium}
}

@article{vaccarinobremner2025relativized,
  author = "Vaccarino Bremner, Sabina",
  title = "The Relativized A Priori, the Historical A Priori, and the Symbolic Form",
  journal = "Philosophy Compass",
  year = "2025",
  volume = "20",
  number = "6",
  doi = "10.1111/phc3.70044",
  abstract = {Recent work in philosophy of science has suggested that scientific paradigms in the wake of revolutions can be conceived as relativized a priori frameworks. This paper puts these accounts in dialog with two accounts of broadly 'cultural' accounts of the relativized a priori in the history of philosophy: Ernst Cassirer's account of symbolic forms, on which the general 'categories' stay the same but their expressions change, and Michel Foucault's account of the historical a priori, which is more thoroughly relativized than Cassirer's. The paper concludes that Foucault can offer helpful resources for philosophy of science insofar as the historical a priori admits of internal tensions that account for the possibility of a transition to a new a priori, and Foucault makes a similar, but more fully developed, appeal to social power than contemporary philosophy of science does in order to make sense of the changes between frameworks. Cassirer, on the other hand, helpfully puts emphasis on the cognitive activity of the subject, rather than on the constitutive a priori principles themselves.},
  abstract_source = {OpenAlex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: This survey article maps the philosophical lineage connecting the logical
  empiricist "relativized a priori" (Reichenbach, Carnap), Cassirer's neo-Kantian "symbolic
  form," and Foucault's "historical a priori," arguing that these represent related but distinct
  strategies for reconciling the historically variable character of frameworks of knowledge with
  the a priori's traditional claim to necessity and universality.

  RELEVANCE: Situates Foucault's episteme and historical a priori within a broader philosophy-of-
  science lineage of relativized-a-priori theorizing, giving the discontinuity debate a
  comparative frame that clarifies what kind of relativism (if any) Foucault's historical a
  priori actually commits him to.

  POSITION: Comparative/expository; treats Foucault's historical a priori as one instance of a
  family of relativized-a-priori strategies rather than as a sui generis or simply relativist
  doctrine.
  },
  keywords = {historical-a-priori, episteme, relativism, discontinuity, Medium}
}

@article{cutrofello2003completeness,
  author = "Cutrofello, Andrew",
  title = "The Completeness of {F}oucault's Table of the Classical Episteme",
  journal = "Philosophy Today",
  year = "2003",
  volume = "47",
  number = "Supplement",
  pages = "56--62",
  doi = "10.5840/philtoday200347supplement8",
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Cutrofello examines the formal, quasi-structuralist "table" by which Foucault
  organizes the classical episteme in The Order of Things (the grid of relations among general
  grammar, natural history, and the analysis of wealth), asking whether this table is complete
  in its own terms or whether it harbors gaps and asymmetries that complicate Foucault's claim to
  have identified a single, internally coherent configuration of classical knowledge.

  RELEVANCE: A technical, close-reading engagement with the internal coherence of Foucault's
  discontinuity thesis at the level of its most schematic articulation, complementing Maclean's
  external historical critique with an internal formal one.

  POSITION: Immanent formal critique; probes the completeness/coherence of Foucault's episteme
  construction on its own methodological terms.
  },
  keywords = {episteme, discontinuity, archaeology, Low, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{tremain2015historicist,
  author = "Tremain, Shelley",
  title = "This is What a Historicist and Relativist Feminist Philosophy of Disability Looks Like",
  journal = "Foucault Studies",
  year = "2015",
  number = "19",
  doi = "10.22439/fs.v0i19.4822",
  abstract = {This article advances a historicist and relativist feminist philosophy of disability, arguing that Foucault's insights offer the most astute tools with which to engage in this intellectual enterprise. Genealogy, the technique of investigation that Friedrich Nietzsche famously introduced and that Foucault took up and adapted in his own work, demonstrates that Foucault's historicist approach has greater explanatory power and transgressive potential for analyses of disability than his critics in disability studies have thus far recognized. The article shows how a feminist philosophy of disability that employs Foucault's technique of genealogy avoids ahistorical, teleological, and transcultural assumptions that beleaguer much work in disability studies, and situates feminist philosophical work on disability squarely in age-old debates in (Eurocentric) Western philosophy about universalism vs. relativism, materialism vs. idealism, realism vs. nominalism, and freewill vs. determinism, as well as contributing to ongoing discussions in (Western) feminist philosophy and theory about essentialism vs. constructivism, identity, race, sexuality, agency, and experience.},
  abstract_source = {OpenAlex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Tremain advances an explicitly historicist and relativist feminist philosophy
  of disability built on Foucauldian genealogy, arguing that this approach avoids the ahistorical,
  teleological, and transcultural assumptions that beset much existing disability theory. She
  situates the argument within the long-standing philosophical debates over universalism versus
  relativism, materialism versus idealism, and realism versus nominalism, contending that
  Foucault's tools are best suited to this terrain precisely because they refuse ahistorical
  essences.

  RELEVANCE: Shows what it looks like to affirmatively adopt, rather than merely defend,
  Foucauldian historicist relativism as a first-order methodological commitment in applied
  genealogical inquiry, providing a concrete test case for whether the relativism charge
  (Taylor, Nola) is a liability or, from within a Foucauldian framework, a resource.

  POSITION: Affirmative application of Foucauldian historicist relativism/nominalism against
  realist and universalist alternatives in disability theory.
  },
  keywords = {relativism, nominalism, historicism, application, Low}
}
